desc: Spectral Vowel Passage - voice<->wash capture instrument, paced per slot
// =====================================================================
// Capture-based vocal instrument. Feeds on LIVE audio playing into it (drag a
// WAV onto the track, or send another track in -- the proper REAPER way).
// Capture a moment into a slot; it's analyzed two ways at once:
//   * harmonic (voice) -- pitch-detected additive resynth: keeps the vowel
//   * spectral (wash)   -- PaulStretch-style phase randomization: breathy bed
// TEXTURE crossfades between them (0 = voice, 100 = wash). Capture up to 8
// slots and MORPH between them. Captures persist across save/reopen.
//
// USE: put audio on the track. Input level up / Voice down -> you hear source.
// When you hear the moment, hit Capture. (Then sweep CAPTURE POINT by ear to
// land exactly on the vowel -- it re-analyzes live, compensating for the
// fraction of a second between hearing and pressing.) Pull Input down, Voice
// up, set Texture, and Morph between slots.
//
//   Capture point = where in the grab to analyze (0 = earliest .. 100 = at press).
//                   PER SLOT: each slot remembers its own point, so scrubbing one
//                   slot never retunes the others. Switching Capture slot shows
//                   that slot's saved point.
//   Texture       = 0 voice (harmonic) .. 100 wash (phase-randomized)
//   Wash grain   = short = rough/grainy, long = glassy/smooth (wash character)
//   Spread        = spectral blur (Hz);  Audition = monitor focused slot / morph
//   Auto-morph    = Off / Sweep (back+forth) / Glide once (first->last slot) / Shuffle (random-order wander)
//   Drift         = suite-wide automation replacement: pick a parameter, and it
//                   wanders on its own (up/down amounts + a wall-clock period),
//                   so a mouse-free / screen-reader user gets evolving motion
//                   without drawing REAPER automation. All targets drift at once.
// =====================================================================

// --- Controls are grouped by what they belong to, not by when they were added.
//     Renumbered several times during the build (at birth, when Slot crossfade
//     was added, when Slot mute joined, and again for the per-slot timing
//     redesign that became "Passage"). Renumbering was free while the plugin
//     had no shipped saved projects -- a Morpher project regenerates onto
//     Passage via tools/morpher_to_passage.py, which maps by label rather than
//     by slider number. Now that Passage is a named, shippable plugin, IDs are
//     primary keys and any further additions APPEND at the end.
//
//     That "no shipped saved projects" assumption expired before it was written
//     down: real Passage projects already existed when Fade in/out shape was
//     inserted at 10-11, and pushing everything from Input level up by two
//     scrambles them on load (Wash grain 150 lands on Voice level dB, and so
//     on). The captures themselves are safe either way -- @serialize is a memory
//     blob and has no idea what a slider number is -- so the fix is a text
//     migration of the project's slider line, NOT another renumber here:
//     tools/passage_migrate_sliders.py, which knows every hop the layout has
//     taken and applies whichever ones a given project still needs (the
//     fade-shape insert at 10-11, then Capture average at 4), seeding each new
//     slider to whatever reproduces the OLD behaviour -- Linear fades, and a
//     one-frame capture average. Run it on any project saved before this
//     build; running it twice does the same as running it once. ---
slider1:1<0,8,1{All,Slot 1,Slot 2,Slot 3,Slot 4,Slot 5,Slot 6,Slot 7,Slot 8}>Capture slot
slider2:0<0,1,1{Off,Capture now}>Capture now
slider3:0<0,100,1>Capture point (%, earliest .. at press)
// --- Capture average: how many analysis frames the WASH spectrum is averaged
//     over, sitting with the other two capture-analysis controls because it is
//     one of them -- it changes how the grab is READ, not how it sounds later.
//     PER SLOT, like Capture point, because it is a property of the material in
//     that slot: a natural sustained vowel wants 1, a slot holding already-
//     processed material wants more.
//
//     1 is the original behaviour and the default, so nothing that exists
//     changes. A single frame freezes that one frame's per-bin scatter and
//     replays it on every grain -- inaudible on natural voice, where successive
//     frames look alike, but a wobble on material that has already been through
//     a spectral process, where the scatter is irregular and its repetition
//     becomes the sound. Averaging several frames lets the scatter cancel while
//     the real spectral shape survives.
//
//     The cost is time-smear: the frames span a stretch of the grab, so a vowel
//     that MOVES gets blended across the average. That is why this is a dial
//     rather than something applied automatically -- turn it up until the wobble
//     goes, no further. Voice (harmonic) analysis is untouched: it resynthesises
//     exact harmonics with continuous phase and has no per-bin scatter to freeze.
slider4:1<1,6,1>Capture average (frames, 1 = one frame)
// --- The SLOT-TIMING cluster (sliders 5-10). All PER SLOT, edited for whichever
//     Capture slot is selected, and laid out in the order a slot lives its
//     life: fade in, hold, fade out, the gap of silence after it, whether it
//     crossfades into the next slot, and mute. Modelled on a DAW item's own
//     fades so the numbers you type are the seconds you hear -- no arithmetic,
//     nothing about a slot living on a different slot. ---
//
// All six controls are LIVE -- the audio engine consumes fade-in, hold,
// fade-out, gap and the crossfade toggle (see the leg-envelope block in
// @sample). With crossfade ON the slot holds, then blends into the next over
// its fade-out, and the gap is unused; with crossfade OFF the slot fades in,
// holds, fades out to silence, then the gap of silence plays before the next
// fades in.
slider12:1<0,1000,0.1>Slot fade in (in slot timing units)
slider13:4<0,1000,0.1>Slot hold (in slot timing units)
slider14:1<0,1000,0.1>Slot fade out (in slot timing units)
slider15:0<0,1000,0.1>Slot gap after (in slot timing units)
slider11:0<0,2,1{Seconds,Hz,Beats}>Slot timing unit
// Crossfade into next: On = the fades overlap, so this slot hands over by
// blending into the next (the classic morph -- what happens today). Off = this
// slot fades out, the gap of silence plays, then the next fades in.
slider18:1<0,1,1{Off,On}>Slot crossfade into next
// --- Slot mute: PER SLOT. When On, the slot is skipped in Sweep / Glide /
//     Shuffle -- the morph moves directly to the next unmuted slot. Focused
//     mode ignores mute (it always plays the slot you point at). ---
slider19:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>Slot mute (skipped in the morph)
// --- Fade CURVE (shape) for the leg fades. GLOBAL -- one setting for ALL
//     slots, sat here with the timing cluster it shapes (the per-slot fade
//     TIMES are 4 and 6 above; this is the curve those fades follow). Same
//     shapes/math as the sweeping-filter suite's Attack/Release Shape.
//     Default Cosine: a smooth eased fade at both ends; Linear is the old
//     straight ramp. Fade-out shape governs the crossfade-OFF fall to
//     silence; crossfade-ON handovers are the spectral crossfade, untouched
//     by these. Global on purpose -- a house style, not a per-moment knob. ---
slider16:1<0,3,1{Linear,Cosine,Logarithmic,Exponential}>Fade in shape (all slots)
slider17:1<0,3,1{Linear,Cosine,Logarithmic,Exponential}>Fade out shape (all slots)
slider60:0<-60,24,0.01>Input level (dry, dB, all slots)
// Named "Voice level" since this plugin was born, and wrong the whole time: it
// is applied to voutL/voutR, the equal-power crossfade of the voice AND the
// wash, so it has always scaled both. PER SLOT here, unlike the Morpher's --
// the morph crossfades it along with the spectra. Relabelled, not re-scoped:
// the behaviour is right, the name described half of it.
slider61:0<-60,24,0.01>Output level (dB)
slider20:50<0,100,1>Texture (% wash)
slider21:150<5,1000,1>Wash grain (ms)
// --- Spread, a pitch block since 2026-09-16 (the Morpher's shape, per slot here). Rozaya, on the
//     Morpher: "For that reason alone we should have a spread unit, and it should encorperate the
//     usual pitch block." No note name: "it's just semitones worth of width." Hz with no fine
//     tune is exactly the blur it always was. In Semitones or Cents each frequency blurs that
//     interval either side; a fine tune in Hz then widens both sides by that many Hz.
slider31:0<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Spread pitch mode
slider32:0<0,1000,0.1>Spread value (Hz / semitones / cents)
slider33:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Spread fine tune unit
slider34:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Spread fine tune
slider22:0<0,128,1{None,C-1,C#-1,D-1,D#-1,E-1,F-1,F#-1,G-1,G#-1,A-1,A#-1,B-1,C0,C#0,D0,D#0,E0,F0,F#0,G0,G#0,A0,A#0,B0,C1,C#1,D1,D#1,E1,F1,F#1,G1,G#1,A1,A#1,B1,C2,C#2,D2,D#2,E2,F2,F#2,G2,G#2,A2,A#2,B2,C3,C#3,D3,D#3,E3,F3,F#3,G3,G#3,A3,A#3,B3,C4,C#4,D4,D#4,E4,F4,F#4,G4,G#4,A4,A#4,B4,C5,C#5,D5,D#5,E5,F5,F#5,G5,G#5,A5,A#5,B5,C6,C#6,D6,D#6,E6,F6,F#6,G6,G#6,A6,A#6,B6,C7,C#7,D7,D#7,E7,F7,F#7,G7,G#7,A7,A#7,B7,C8,C#8,D8,D#8,E8,F8,F#8,G8,G#8,A8,A#8,B8,C9,C#9,D9,D#9,E9,F9,F#9,G9}>Pitch source note (where zero is)
slider24:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Pitch source fine tune (only with a Pitch source note, in Semitones)
slider23:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Pitch source fine tune unit
slider25:60<0,127,1{C-1,C#-1,D-1,D#-1,E-1,F-1,F#-1,G-1,G#-1,A-1,A#-1,B-1,C0,C#0,D0,D#0,E0,F0,F#0,G0,G#0,A0,A#0,B0,C1,C#1,D1,D#1,E1,F1,F#1,G1,G#1,A1,A#1,B1,C2,C#2,D2,D#2,E2,F2,F#2,G2,G#2,A2,A#2,B2,C3,C#3,D3,D#3,E3,F3,F#3,G3,G#3,A3,A#3,B3,C4,C#4,D4,D#4,E4,F4,F#4,G4,G#4,A4,A#4,B4,C5,C#5,D5,D#5,E5,F5,F#5,G5,G#5,A5,A#5,B5,C6,C#6,D6,D#6,E6,F6,F#6,G6,G#6,A6,A#6,B6,C7,C#7,D7,D#7,E7,F7,F#7,G7,G#7,A7,A#7,B7,C8,C#8,D8,D#8,E8,F8,F#8,G8,G#8,A8,A#8,B8,C9,C#9,D9,D#9,E9,F9,F#9,G9}>Pitch target note (only with a Pitch source note, in Semitones)
slider27:0<-20000,20000,0.001>Pitch transpose value (Hz / semitones / cents)
slider26:1<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Pitch transpose mode
slider29:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Pitch fine tune
slider28:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Pitch fine tune unit
slider30:440<20,2000,0.1>Tuning reference (Hz, all slots)
slider35:50<0,100,1>Stereo width (%)
// --- Low cut and High cut, per-slot pitch blocks since 2026-09-16 (the Morpher's). 0 means OFF in
//     every unit, and each note list starts on a dedicated Off (Rozaya: "Feels like 0 could just
//     be 0, then the first thing everything lands on is a dedicated off position"). In Semitones
//     the value is a note number (69 = A4 at the Tuning reference), in Cents that times 100. The
//     note name and the value follow each other; switching the mode converts the value. A drift
//     or ramp on a cut that is ON stops at the lowest real frequency rather than switching it
//     off, and moves nothing while it is off. High cut's off was 20000 before this.
slider37:0<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Low cut pitch mode
slider38:0<0,128,1{Off,C-1,C#-1,D-1,D#-1,E-1,F-1,F#-1,G-1,G#-1,A-1,A#-1,B-1,C0,C#0,D0,D#0,E0,F0,F#0,G0,G#0,A0,A#0,B0,C1,C#1,D1,D#1,E1,F1,F#1,G1,G#1,A1,A#1,B1,C2,C#2,D2,D#2,E2,F2,F#2,G2,G#2,A2,A#2,B2,C3,C#3,D3,D#3,E3,F3,F#3,G3,G#3,A3,A#3,B3,C4,C#4,D4,D#4,E4,F4,F#4,G4,G#4,A4,A#4,B4,C5,C#5,D5,D#5,E5,F5,F#5,G5,G#5,A5,A#5,B5,C6,C#6,D6,D#6,E6,F6,F#6,G6,G#6,A6,A#6,B6,C7,C#7,D7,D#7,E7,F7,F#7,G7,G#7,A7,A#7,B7,C8,C#8,D8,D#8,E8,F8,F#8,G8,G#8,A8,A#8,B8,C9,C#9,D9,D#9,E9,F9,F#9,G9}>Low cut note name
slider39:0<0,20000,0.001>Low cut value (0 = off)
slider40:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>Low cut fine tune unit
slider41:0<-1000,1000,0.001>Low cut fine tune
slider42:0<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>High cut pitch mode
slider43:0<0,128,1{Off,C-1,C#-1,D-1,D#-1,E-1,F-1,F#-1,G-1,G#-1,A-1,A#-1,B-1,C0,C#0,D0,D#0,E0,F0,F#0,G0,G#0,A0,A#0,B0,C1,C#1,D1,D#1,E1,F1,F#1,G1,G#1,A1,A#1,B1,C2,C#2,D2,D#2,E2,F2,F#2,G2,G#2,A2,A#2,B2,C3,C#3,D3,D#3,E3,F3,F#3,G3,G#3,A3,A#3,B3,C4,C#4,D4,D#4,E4,F4,F#4,G4,G#4,A4,A#4,B4,C5,C#5,D5,D#5,E5,F5,F#5,G5,G#5,A5,A#5,B5,C6,C#6,D6,D#6,E6,F6,F#6,G6,G#6,A6,A#6,B6,C7,C#7,D7,D#7,E7,F7,F#7,G7,G#7,A7,A#7,B7,C8,C#8,D8,D#8,E8,F8,F#8,G8,G#8,A8,A#8,B8,C9,C#9,D9,D#9,E9,F9,F#9,G9}>High cut note name
slider44:0<0,20000,0.001>High cut value (0 = off)
slider45:2<0,2,1{Hz,Semitones,Cents}>High cut fine tune unit
slider46:0<-1000,1000,0.001>High cut fine tune
slider36:0<0,100,0.1>Denoise (%, wash only)
slider5:1<0,1,1{Focused slot,Morph}>Audition (all slots)
slider6:0<0,100,0.1>Morph (% across captured slots)
slider7:0<0,3,1{Off,Sweep (back+forth),Glide once,Shuffle (random)}>Auto-morph (all slots)
slider63:0<0,1000,0.001>Start delay (in transport units, all slots)
slider64:0<0,1000,0.001>Play for (in transport units, 0 = always, all slots)
slider65:0<0,1000,0.001>Rest for (in transport units, 0 = always, all slots)
slider62:0<0,2,1{Seconds,Hz,Beats}>Transport unit (all slots)
slider66:0<0,1,1{Walk through,Freeze in place}>Auto-morph rest mode (all slots)
slider67:0<0,1,1{Pass-through,Silence}>Output at rest (all slots)
// --- Drift: suite-wide automation replacement (nested-selector pattern).
//     Pick a target; it wanders by up/down amounts over a wall-clock period.
//     All targets drift in parallel; the selector only chooses which one the
//     amount/period/shape sliders are editing. ---
slider68:0<0,21,1{Transpose,Fine tune,Texture,Wash grain,Spread,Denoise,Low cut,High cut,Overtone harmonic,Overtone lift,Slot fade in,Slot hold,Slot fade out,Slot gap after,Stereo width,Output level,Tuning reference,Overtone width,Morph,Input level,Play for,Rest for}>Drift target
slider70:0<0,20000,0.01>Drift up amount (in the Drift amount unit)
slider71:0<0,20000,0.01>Drift down amount (in the Drift amount unit)
slider69:0<0,12,1{Target default,Hz,Semitones,Cents,Milliseconds,Seconds,Minutes,BPM,Beats,Cycles,dB,Percent,Degrees}>Drift amount unit (Target default where it cannot fit)
slider73:30<0,1000,0.001>Drift period (in drift period units, 0 = off)
slider72:1<0,2,1{Cycles,Seconds,Beats}>Drift period unit (all targets)
// WHETHER A DRIFT MOVES WITH ITS OWN THING, OR ON A CLOCK (2026-09-12, Bubbler's control).
// The four slot timings are read once, when their slot's leg begins. `With the target` steps
// such a drift on that slot's own legs, by each leg's length in the period unit, and holds
// it between them; `On a clock` runs it all the time. Every other target is read all the
// time, so its turn is every sample and the two are the same. Rozaya: "Um. yes?"
slider74:0<0,1,1{With the target,On a clock}>Drift movement mode
slider75:0<0,2,1{Sine,Triangle,Random}>Drift shape
slider76:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift play for (periods, 0 = always)
slider77:0<0,1000,0.01>Drift rest for (periods, 0 = always)
// 2026-09-16, the Morpher's R27: a transport rest freezes the drift only if this says so.
// Walk through is what every saved copy did.
slider78:0<0,1,1{Walk through,Freeze in place}>Drift rest mode
// Restart on play = drift jumps to the start of its cycle when the transport
// begins (so multiple tracks reset together and stay in sync). Free-running =
// drift ignores the transport and evolves continuously (loop under/over an
// unbroken drift). A loop repeat is never a restart in either mode.
slider79:0<0,1,1{Restart on play,Free-running}>Drift restart (all targets)
// --- Ramp: the suite's "Speed Ramp" under an honest name (this plugin has no
//     speed to ramp). A one-time slow RIDE of a parameter: pick a target, how
//     far to move it (signed) and over how long, arm Engage, and it glides there
//     once and holds. Nested-selector like Drift; all targets ride in parallel
//     off one Engage; composes with Drift (base + drift wander + ramp ride). ---
slider80:0<0,21,1{Transpose,Fine tune,Texture,Wash grain,Spread,Denoise,Low cut,High cut,Overtone harmonic,Overtone lift,Slot fade in,Slot hold,Slot fade out,Slot gap after,Stereo width,Output level,Tuning reference,Overtone width,Morph,Input level,Play for,Rest for}>Ramp target
slider82:0<-20000,20000,0.001>Ramp by (in the Ramp by unit)
slider81:0<0,12,1{Target default,Hz,Semitones,Cents,Milliseconds,Seconds,Minutes,BPM,Beats,Cycles,dB,Percent,Degrees}>Ramp by unit (Target default where it cannot fit)
slider83:2<0,3,1{Cycles,Seconds,Minutes,Beats}>Ramp time unit (all targets)
slider84:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp duration (in ramp time units)
slider85:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp play for (0 = smooth)
slider86:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp rest for (0 = smooth)
// 2026-09-16, as Drift rest mode, for the ramps.
slider87:0<0,1,1{Walk through,Freeze in place}>Ramp rest mode
slider89:0<0,1000,0.01>Ramp start delay (in ramp time units)
slider88:0<0,1,1{Off,On}>Ramp engage (all targets)
// --- Overtone: bring ONE partial of the voice out far enough to be heard as a
//     note of its own, the way overtone / throat singing does. Appended at the
//     end -- these are a new block belonging to nothing above them, so there is
//     no grouping argument for an insert and no migration to pay for.
//
//     The physics it models: a singer does not add a second voice. They hold one
//     fundamental and narrow the vocal tract into a sharp resonance parked on one
//     harmonic of that same series, so that partial stands 20-30 dB clear of its
//     neighbours and the ear hears it as a separate pitch. The melody is walking
//     the resonance up and down the series, which is why overtone melodies only
//     ever land on harmonic-series intervals.
//
//     So this is modelled as ATTENUATION of the others, never a boost of the
//     chosen one. A tract resonance redistributes energy, it does not add any --
//     and modelled this way the control can only ever make the voice quieter,
//     which matters when the person reaching for it is wearing headphones and
//     cannot see the meter. A boost slider at these depths would be an ear-safety
//     problem.
//
//     Harmonic 1 is exempt, always. It is the drone half of "two notes at once":
//     suppress it and you have a whistle, not overtone singing.
//
//     Works at BOTH ends of Texture, and the character follows the engine. On the
//     voice it isolates one exact partial: a clean, near-pure tone. On the wash --
//     where phase has been randomised, so every partial is already a narrow band
//     of noise -- the same window leaves a pitched BAND: breathier, airier, still
//     a definite note. Same gesture, different material, which is what Texture
//     means everywhere else in this plugin. Both engines read the same smoothed
//     controls and the same blended fundamental, so they stay in step through a
//     Texture blend instead of describing two different overtones.
slider47:0<0,64,1>Overtone harmonic (0 = off, 1 = fundamental)
slider48:24<0,48,0.01>Overtone lift (dB the chosen harmonic rises by)
slider49:1<0.5,4,0.1>Overtone width (all slots, harmonics either side)

@init
// ext_noinit = 1: @init runs ONLY on plugin load, so captured slots (and all
// memory) survive transport stop/play -- the whole point of a capture-based
// instrument. RUNTIME state (accumulators, positions, smoother) is reset
// explicitly on the transport play-edge in @block instead, so playback still
// restarts cleanly (and renders correctly) without wiping the captures.
ext_noinit = 1;
TWOPI = 6.283185307179586; PI = 3.141592653589793;
NSLOTS = 8; MAXFFT = 32768; INRING = 65536;
N_TARGETS = 22;  // Drift/Ramp targets in CONTROL ORDER (2026-09-12, docs/layouts/spectral-vowel-passage.md):
                 // per slot 0 Transpose, 1 Fine tune, 2 Texture, 3 Wash grain, 4 Spread, 5 Denoise,
                 // 6 Low cut, 7 High cut, 8 Overtone harmonic, 9 Overtone lift, 10 Slot fade in,
                 // 11 Slot hold, 12 Slot fade out, 13 Slot gap after, 14 Stereo width, 15 Output level;
                 // whole plugin (held in slot 0's row) 16 Tuning reference, 17 Overtone width,
                 // 18 Morph, 19 Input level, 20 Play for, 21 Rest for.
                 // It was 14 (0 Texture, 1 Spread, 2 Pitch, 3 Stereo width, 4 Low cut, 5 Output
                 // level, 6 Denoise, 7 Morph, 8 Input level, 9 Overtone harmonic, 10-13 the slot
                 // timings); tg_o2n() maps those, and a save or a slider line is remapped.
TG_GLOBAL = 16;  // targets from here on are the whole plugin's and live at slot 0
// Wash grain rebuilds a window rather than scaling a value, so its drift is read
// once a block in @block, not per sample.
FFTSIZE = 32768; NBINS = 16385;
// WA = analysis window length, FIXED and decoupled from Wash grain. The capture
// analysis (heavy: FFT + YIN) happens at WA; Wash grain only rebuilds the cheap
// synthesis grain. That makes Wash grain safe to AUTOMATE (no re-FFT, no buffer
// clear -> no dropouts), and Capture point scrubs this WA-length window in the grab.
WA = 8192;   // ~170 ms @ 48k: good spectral resolution, leaves ~0.5 s scrub range
// Capture average (the wash's per-slot frame count). Frames step by half a
// window, so successive frames see genuinely different scatter while still
// overlapping enough to describe the same moment. CAPAVG_MAX is set by what
// fits inside one grab: the frames span WA + (K-1)*CAPAVG_HOP samples, and at
// K = 6 that is 28672 of the 32768-sample grab, leaving room for the capture
// point to still choose WHERE inside the grab the average sits.
CAPAVG_HOP = WA / 2; CAPAVG_MAX = 6;
// Wash grain reaches its control's 1000 ms at ANY sample rate (2026-09-13). It stopped at
// MAXFFT samples -- 743 ms at 44.1 kHz, 680 at 48, 341 at 96 -- because a grain was at most
// one FFTSIZE inverse FFT, and JSFX's fft() stops at 32768 (REAPER's documentation; a round
// trip at 65536 measured silently wrong). A longer grain is now built from FFTSIZE pieces
// half-overlapping inside it, each with its own random phases (the Morpher's build); a
// grain that fits one piece is built exactly as before. GMAX samples is one second.
// Rozaya: "sounds good".
GMAX = ceil(srate);
// ACCLEN must exceed the LONGEST grain plus a hop, or a long grain wraps the circular
// accumulator onto the read pointer. 65536 still covers a second at 44.1 and 48 kHz.
ACCLEN = 65536; loop(8, ACCLEN < GMAX + GMAX/4 + 1 ? ACCLEN *= 2; );
NHARM = 64; WHARM = 3072;          // 64 harmonics; ~64 ms YIN analysis window
WTBL = 8192; WTSCALE = WTBL / TWOPI;
// Voice stereo width: at full Stereo width the right-channel voice runs +-0.8%
// (~14 cents) detuned from the left, so L/R beat slowly -> width + de-robot
// shimmer. Tune by ear; bigger = wider/chorus-ier, smaller = tighter.
VOICE_DETUNE_MAX = 0.008;

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Memory map: a RUNNING allocator, not hand-picked addresses. Every buffer is
// sized from the constants above, so NSLOTS (or any window size) can change
// without hand-editing offsets. The old fixed-address map collided the moment
// NSLOTS went past 4 (slotraw ran straight into slot_norm) -- this cannot.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
freemem = 0;
fftwork   = freemem; freemem += 2*FFTSIZE;    // FFT scratch (interleaved re/im)
inbuf     = freemem; freemem += INRING;        // live input ring
window    = freemem; freemem += WA;            // Hann of length WA (fixed analysis window)
synwin    = freemem; freemem += GMAX;          // sqrt-Hann synthesis grain (length W = Wash grain, up to 1 s)
accL      = freemem; freemem += ACCLEN;        // overlap-add accumulator L
accR      = freemem; freemem += ACCLEN;        // overlap-add accumulator R
mtmp      = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
curmag    = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
phase     = freemem; freemem += NBINS;
doff      = freemem; freemem += NBINS;         // per-bin stereo decorrelation offsets
grainbuf  = freemem; freemem += GMAX;          // a grain, left (and right, when a short grain reads it from fftwork)
grainR    = freemem; freemem += GMAX;          // a long grain's right channel, assembled piece by piece
// A long grain's piece window: periodic sqrt-Hann, so two pieces half a piece apart keep
// the power constant where they overlap (their squares sum to exactly 1). No rand().
pwin      = freemem; freemem += FFTSIZE;
pw_i = 0; loop(FFTSIZE, pwin[pw_i] = sqrt(0.5 - 0.5*cos(6.283185307179586*pw_i/FFTSIZE)); pw_i += 1; );
// --- per-slot capture stores (ALL sized by NSLOTS -- this is what makes the
//     slot count a free dial) ---
slotmag   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*NBINS;  // wash (spectral) magnitudes
slotraw   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*MAXFFT; // raw captured audio (source of truth)
slot_norm = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // wash peak normalization
slot_harm = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS*NHARM;  // voice harmonic magnitudes
slot_f0   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // detected fundamentals (Hz)
slot_hnorm= freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // voice peak normalization
slot_voicedb  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT voice_db_base
slot_texture  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT texture
slot_spread   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT spread_hz
slot_pitch    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT pitch_semi
slot_width    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT stereo_width
slot_lowcut   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT lowcut_hz
slot_denoise  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // PER-SLOT denoise_amt
// slot_linger holds the "Slot hold" seconds; slot_xfade holds "Slot fade out"
// seconds. (Internal names kept from before the Half-1 relabel so the serialize
// migration stays byte-compatible; the slider LABELS are hold / fade out.)
slot_linger   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // PER-SLOT hold time in SECONDS
slot_xfade    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // PER-SLOT fade-out length in SECONDS
slot_fadein   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // PER-SLOT fade-in length in SECONDS (Half 2 consumes this)
slot_gap      = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // PER-SLOT silence AFTER this slot, in SECONDS (Half 2)
slot_xfadeon  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // PER-SLOT crossfade-into-next flag 0/1 (Half 2)
slot_mute     = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // PER-SLOT mute flag (0/1); muted slots are skipped in Sweep / Glide / Shuffle
active_slots  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;      // indices of unmuted slots (0..n_used-1 minus muted ones); rebuilt every @block
slot_cappoint = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // PER-SLOT capture point (0..1): where in each slot's grab to analyze. Each slot remembers its own scrub position.
slot_ot_harm  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // PER-SLOT Overtone harmonic (0 = none for this slot)
slot_ot_depth = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // PER-SLOT Overtone lift (dB the chosen partial rises by). Name kept for the @serialize field; the meaning inverted 2026-07-28.
slot_capavg   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;    // PER-SLOT capture average (1..CAPAVG_MAX frames) for the WASH spectrum. 1 = the original single-frame analysis.
// --- harmonic (voice) engine scratch ---
hph       = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // resynth phase accumulators (voice A, left)
hphB      = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // resynth phase accumulators (voice B, left)
hphR      = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // voice A, right channel (detuned for width)
hphBR     = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // voice B, right channel (detuned for width)
ot_gain   = freemem; freemem += NHARM;         // per-harmonic Overtone gain multiplier (1 = untouched); rebuilt only when the overtone controls move
anbuf     = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // mono analysis frame
yin_df    = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // YIN difference function
yin_cmnd  = freemem; freemem += WHARM;         // YIN cumulative-mean-normalized difference
// --- sine wavetable (render-speed optimization): the voice engine's per-sample
// sin() calls become a table lookup. Internal only -- no sliders, no pitch or
// behavior change (frequency is set by the phase INCREMENT, which is untouched). ---
sintab    = freemem; freemem += WTBL+2;        // one sine period + a guard sample for interp
driftbag  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;        // Auto-morph Shuffle bag (even random slot coverage)
// --- suite Drift: config + runtime banks, indexed PER SLOT x PER TARGET.
//     Passage is a per-slot instrument -- Texture, Pitch, the leg timings and
//     the rest all belong to a slot -- and drift was the last thing in it that
//     was not. It is now keyed DKEY(slot, target) = slot*DSTRIDE + target.
//     DSTRIDE went 16 -> 32 on 2026-09-12 when the list grew to 22; a save at 16
//     is remapped in @serialize (magic 7700007).
//     Targets from TG_GLOBAL on (Tuning reference, Overtone width, Morph, Input
//     level, Play for, Rest for) are GLOBAL and always live at slot 0 -- a per-slot
//     Morph drift would be circular, since Morph decides which slot is playing. ---
DSTRIDE = 32;
DBANK   = DSTRIDE * NSLOTS;
target_drift_up     = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: up amount   (survives transport)
target_drift_down   = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: down amount
target_drift_per    = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: period (seconds)
target_drift_shape  = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: 0 Sine / 1 Triangle / 2 Random
target_drift_phase  = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: 0..1 phase (reset on play)
target_drift_prev   = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: Random shape prev value
target_drift_curr   = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: Random shape target value
target_drift_offset = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: current offset (read at consumption)
td_active           = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // @slider: 1 if a target has any drift configured
// --- Ramp: per-target config + runtime banks (16-slot-spaced) ---
ramp_by_mem            = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: signed `by` amount
ramp_dur_mem           = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: duration (minutes)
ramp_delay_mem         = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // CONFIG: start delay (minutes)
ramp_t_mem             = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: 0..1 ride progress (reset on play)
ramp_delay_elapsed_mem = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: start-delay sample counter
ramp_offset_mem        = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // RUNTIME: ramp_t * by (read at consumption)
mod_active             = freemem; freemem += DBANK;  // @slider: 1 if a target has drift OR ramp configured
// All slots, 2026-09-11 (docs/layouts/spectral-vowel-passage.md). The last VISIBLE
// value of each per-slot control (0-16), Drift setting (20-23) and Ramp setting
// (24-26), so that on All only a control actually moved reaches all eight slots --
// Polyrhythm's change-detected model. Adopted in @block and @serialize, never on a
// first @slider (jsfx-gotchas: an adopt-on-first-@slider flag is the bug).
ps_last                = freemem; freemem += 64;
ps_inited = 0;
// Pitch block, per slot (2026-09-11): Transpose unit, Fine tune and its unit, Source
// note, Source fine tune and its unit, Target note. Transpose VALUE stays in slot_pitch.
// Saved from magic 7700008, appended at the end of @serialize.
slot_tunit    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_fine     = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_funit    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_srcnote  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_srcfine  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_srcfunit = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_tgtnote  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
// Per-slot Wash grain and High cut (2026-09-11). Saved from 7700008.
slot_grain    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_hicut    = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;   // 0 = off since 2026-09-16 (was 20000)
// The Spread and cut pitch blocks (2026-09-16), per slot. Saved from 7700009. The note names are
// not banked: they are always the value's, shown in its mode.
slot_spmode   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_spfunit  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_spfine   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_lcmode   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_lcfunit  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_lcfine   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_hcmode   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_hcfunit  = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
slot_hcfine   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
spc           = freemem; freemem += NBINS + 1;   // Spread in Semitones/Cents: running sums of mtmp
// --- The voice's wavetables (2026-09-16). Two positions (0 = A, the vsi voice; 1 = B, vsj), two
//     tables each (the one playing and the one being faded to), each an ifft buffer of 2*WTN.
//     Built by wt_build; see the voice engine in @sample.
WTN = 8192; WT_XF = 256;
wt_tab   = freemem; freemem += 4;         // table addresses, [p*2 + q]
wt_tab[0] = freemem; freemem += 2*WTN; wt_tab[1] = freemem; freemem += 2*WTN;
wt_tab[2] = freemem; freemem += 2*WTN; wt_tab[3] = freemem; freemem += 2*WTN;
wt_phi   = freemem; freemem += 2*NHARM;   // each phase bank's partial start phases, [bank*NHARM + n-1]
wt_ph    = freemem; freemem += 4;         // running phase in cycles, [p*2 + channel]
wt_act   = freemem; freemem += 2;         // which of the two tables is playing
wt_xf    = freemem; freemem += 2;         // samples left in the fade from the other table
wt_wait  = freemem; freemem += 2;         // samples before the next build may start
wt_bank  = freemem; freemem += 2;         // which start-phase bank the voice's partials use
wt_slot  = freemem; freemem += 2;         // what the playing table was built from ...
wt_otv   = freemem; freemem += 2;
wt_hv    = freemem; freemem += 2;
wt_nb    = freemem; freemem += 2;
wt_f     = freemem; freemem += 2;
wt_hct   = freemem; freemem += 2;
// Per-slot timing unit (2026-09-11), for fade in, hold, fade out and gap: Rozaya's
// {Seconds, Hz, Beats}. In Hz a timing lasts one cycle; 0 still means none (mine --
// settled: Rozaya kept it). In Beats a tempo change lands at once, mid-leg (@sample).
slot_tmunit   = freemem; freemem += NSLOTS;
function tm_sec(u, v) ( u == 1 ? (v > 0.000001 ? 1 / v : 0) : u == 2 ? v * 60 / max(tempo, 1) : v );
grain_seed = 0;
hc_on = 0; hc_top = srate*0.5; hc_lo = 1000000000; hc_inv = 0; hc_bound = srate*0.5;   // EEL2 has no scientific notation
// A partial's High cut gain: 1 below the fade, falling to 0 at the cut.
function hc_g(f) ( f > hc_lo ? (hc_top - f) * hc_inv : 1; );
// Build voice position p's next table from a slot at frequency f, and start fading to it. The
// partials are exactly the sine engine's: magnitude / the slot's peak, the Overtone curve, the High
// cut's fade, each at its start phase; a partial is left out once its right-channel copy (the higher
// of the two) would reach hc_bound. JSFX's ifft does not scale, so each bin holds half the amplitude.
function wt_build(p, slot, f) local(q, b, hn, nrm, a, ps, re, im, fr, pb) (
  q = 1 - wt_act[p]; b = wt_tab[p*2 + q]; pb = wt_bank[p] * NHARM;
  memset(b, 0, 2*WTN);
  nrm = slot_hnorm[slot]; nrm <= 0 ? nrm = 1;
  fr = f * (1 + voice_detune);
  hn = 1;
  loop(NHARM,
    hn * fr < hc_bound ? (
      a = slot_harm[slot*NHARM + hn - 1] / nrm * ot_gain[hn - 1] * hc_g(hn * f) * 0.5;
      ps = wt_phi[pb + hn - 1] - PI * 0.5;
      re = a * cos(ps); im = a * sin(ps);
      b[2*hn] = re; b[2*hn + 1] = im; b[2*(WTN - hn)] = re; b[2*(WTN - hn) + 1] = -im;
    );
    hn += 1;
  );
  fft_ipermute(b, WTN); ifft(b, WTN);
  wt_act[p] = q; wt_xf[p] = wt_slot[p] < 0 ? 0 : WT_XF; wt_wait[p] = WT_XF;
  wt_slot[p] = slot; wt_otv[p] = ot_ver; wt_hv[p] = harm_ver; wt_f[p] = f; wt_hct[p] = hc_on ? hc_top : 0;
  wt_nb[p] = fr > 0 ? floor(hc_bound / fr) : NHARM + 1;
);
// Rebuild position p's table when what it holds has changed: another slot, a capture, the Overtone
// curve, the High cut, a partial crossing Nyquist, or -- only while the High cut is fading some
// partial -- the pitch moving by more than a fifth of a cent's worth of ratio.
function wt_check(p, slot, f) local(fr, nb) (
  wt_wait[p] > 0 ? wt_wait[p] -= 1;
  fr = f * (1 + voice_detune); nb = fr > 0 ? floor(hc_bound / fr) : NHARM + 1;
  (wt_slot[p] != slot || wt_otv[p] != ot_ver || wt_hv[p] != harm_ver || wt_hct[p] != (hc_on ? hc_top : 0) ||
   min(nb, NHARM + 1) != min(wt_nb[p], NHARM + 1) ||
   (hc_on && NHARM * f > hc_lo && abs(f - wt_f[p]) > 0.0001 * wt_f[p])) && (wt_wait[p] <= 0 || wt_slot[p] < 0) ? wt_build(p, slot, f);
);
// One channel of position p, advancing its phase by f / srate. Reads both tables while fading.
// Position p takes position o's voice whole: phases, start-phase bank, playing table and its stamps.
// Nothing is rebuilt, so the handover is as seamless as the sine engine's phase copy.
function wt_take(p, o) (
  wt_ph[p*2] = wt_ph[o*2]; wt_ph[p*2 + 1] = wt_ph[o*2 + 1]; wt_bank[p] = wt_bank[o];
  memcpy(wt_tab[p*2 + wt_act[p]], wt_tab[o*2 + wt_act[o]], 2*WTN); wt_xf[p] = 0;
  wt_slot[p] = wt_slot[o]; wt_otv[p] = wt_otv[o]; wt_hv[p] = wt_hv[o]; wt_nb[p] = wt_nb[o]; wt_f[p] = wt_f[o]; wt_hct[p] = wt_hct[o];
);
function wt_read(p, ch, f) local(ph, x, i0, fr, t, v, m) (
  m = WTN - 1;
  ph = wt_ph[p*2 + ch]; x = ph * WTN; i0 = floor(x); fr = x - i0;
  t = wt_tab[p*2 + wt_act[p]];
  v = t[2*(i0 & m)] * (1 - fr) + t[2*((i0 + 1) & m)] * fr;
  wt_xf[p] > 0 ? (
    t = wt_tab[p*2 + 1 - wt_act[p]];
    v = v + (t[2*(i0 & m)] * (1 - fr) + t[2*((i0 + 1) & m)] * fr - v) * wt_xf[p] / WT_XF;
  );
  ph += f / srate; ph >= 1 ? ph -= floor(ph);
  wt_ph[p*2 + ch] = ph;
  v;
);
// Transport (2026-09-11), the Morpher's behaviour in Passage's own Transport unit:
// Start delay, then Play for / Rest for. Counters restart on every play edge.
start_delay_elapsed = 0; pr_accum = 0; pr_resting = 0; in_rest = 0;
in_start_delay = 0; mod_frozen = 0; ramp_frozen = 0; morph_frozen = 0;
start_delay_sec = 0; pr_play_sec = 0; pr_rest_sec = 0; pr_enabled = 0; morph_at_rest = 0; out_at_rest = 0;
// Drift and Ramp play/rest per (slot, target), and their counters (2026-09-11, from
// the Morpher). The settings are saved from 7700008; the counters are not.
target_drift_play = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
target_drift_rest = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
drift_pr_accum    = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
drift_pr_resting  = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
ramp_play_mem     = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
ramp_rest_mem     = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
ramp_pr_accum     = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
ramp_pr_resting   = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
// Drift amount unit and Ramp by unit per (slot, target), 2026-09-12. 0 = Target default.
// Saved from 7700008.
target_drift_unit = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
ramp_by_unit      = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
// Drift movement per (slot, target): 0 with the target, 1 on a clock. Saved from 7700008.
target_drift_moves = freemem; freemem += DBANK;
drift_step_len     = freemem; freemem += DBANK;   // RUNTIME: seconds of the leg that just began; 0 = no turn
ser_scratch       = freemem; freemem += DBANK;   // @serialize's remap of an older save
dr_order          = freemem; freemem += DBANK;   // the drift loop's visiting order, below
// Per-sample work lists, rebuilt every block (2026-09-16, the Morpher's): @sample walked every
// (slot, target) drift and ramp entry on every sample whether it moved or not; now only these.
// Skipped entries did nothing when walked, so the output is the same, sample for sample.
drift_list = freemem; freemem += DBANK;   // drifting entries, in dr_order's order (rand() order kept)
ramp_list  = freemem; freemem += DBANK;   // entries with a ramp duration (they advance)
rofs_list  = freemem; freemem += DBANK;   // entries with a ramp amount (their offset can be non-zero)
dl_n = 0; rl_n = 0; ro_n = 0;
// Drift + Ramp on one (slot, target), in the target's own unit.
function mo_q(k) ( target_drift_offset[k] + ramp_offset_mem[k]; );
// (The amount units' conversion is below pv_semis, which it calls: EEL2 needs a function
// defined before its first use.)
// A 14-target save's index, in the 22-target list.
function tg_o2n(o) (
  o == 0 ? 2 : o == 1 ? 4 : o == 2 ? 0 : o == 3 ? 14 : o == 4 ? 6 : o == 5 ? 15 :
  o == 6 ? 5 : o == 7 ? 18 : o == 8 ? 19 : o == 9 ? 8 : o;
);
// Move a bank saved at stride 16 in the old order (nsl slots, ntg targets each) into
// this layout; everything the save did not hold takes dflt.
function tg_remap(bank, dflt, nsl, ntg) local(k, s, t) (
  k = 0; loop(nsl * 16, ser_scratch[k] = bank[k]; k += 1;);
  k = 0; loop(DBANK, bank[k] = dflt; k += 1;);
  s = 0; loop(nsl, t = 0; loop(ntg, bank[s*DSTRIDE + tg_o2n(t)] = ser_scratch[s*16 + t]; t += 1;); s += 1;);
);
// THE DRIFT LOOP VISITS THE OLD FOURTEEN TARGETS FIRST, slot by slot in their old
// order, then the eight new ones. Random drift draws rand(), one stream, so two
// targets wrapping on one sample must draw in the order they always did (the Morpher's
// rule).
dro_k = 0; dro_s = 0;
loop(NSLOTS, dro_t = 0; loop(14, dr_order[dro_k] = dro_s*DSTRIDE + tg_o2n(dro_t); dro_k += 1; dro_t += 1;); dro_s += 1;);
dro_s = 0;
loop(NSLOTS,
  dro_t = 0;
  loop(DSTRIDE,
    dro_old = 0; dro_j = 0; loop(14, tg_o2n(dro_j) == dro_t ? dro_old = 1; dro_j += 1;);
    !dro_old ? ( dr_order[dro_k] = dro_s*DSTRIDE + dro_t; dro_k += 1; );
    dro_t += 1;
  );
  dro_s += 1;
);
// Seconds per Drift period unit and per Ramp time unit, seeded to their declared
// defaults (Seconds, Minutes); @block overwrites both every block.
drift_unit_sec = 1; ramp_unit_sec = 60;
// Shared play/rest window counter: off unless BOTH are > 0; returns 1 while resting.
function pr_frozen(accum_bank, resting_bank, idx, tick, play, rest) (
  play > 0 && rest > 0 ? (
    accum_bank[idx] += tick;
    accum_bank[idx] >= (resting_bank[idx] ? rest : play) ? (
      accum_bank[idx] = 0;
      resting_bank[idx] = 1 - resting_bank[idx];
    );
    resting_bank[idx];
  ) : ( 0; );
);
tuning_ref = 440; tuning_base = 440;   // tuning_ref is the heard one: base plus any Drift or Ramp
// Bubbler's model: every pitch value resolves to SEMITONES. Hz counts from the Tuning
// reference. A Source fine tune in Hz counts from the source note's own frequency.
function pv_semis(u, v) local(r) (
  r = max(tuning_ref, 0.001);
  u == 0 ? 12 * log(max(r + v, 0.001) / r) / log(2) : u == 2 ? v / 100 : v;
);
function pv_src_semis(idx, u, v) local(f) (
  f = max(tuning_ref, 0.001) * pow(2, (idx - 1 - 69) / 12);
  u == 0 ? 12 * log(max(f + v, 0.001) / f) / log(2) : u == 2 ? v / 100 : v;
);
// --- The amount units (2026-09-12). A Drift or Ramp amount is held in the unit its picker
// names, {0 Target default, 1 Hz, 2 Semitones, 3 Cents, 4 Milliseconds, 5 Seconds,
// 6 Minutes, 7 BPM, 8 Beats, 9 Cycles, 10 dB, 11 Percent, 12 Degrees}, and turned into the
// target's OWN unit here, against the value it rides on. A unit that cannot fit the target
// acts as Target default -- Rozaya: "It should fall back to the target's native unit".
// Percent, dB and count targets therefore take every amount as it stands.
//
// A pitch value (Transpose, Fine tune) in nu {0 Hz, 1 Semitones, 2 Cents}, Bubbler's
// meanings: Hz counts from the Tuning reference. An Hz amount moves the heard frequency.
function au_pitch(off, u, base, nu) local(s, s2, r) (
  (u < 1 || u > 3) || (u == 1 && nu == 0) || (u == 2 && nu == 1) || (u == 3 && nu == 2) ? off : (
    r = max(tuning_ref, 0.001);
    s = pv_semis(nu, base);
    s2 = u == 2 ? s + off : u == 3 ? s + off / 100 : 12 * log(max(r * pow(2, s / 12) + off, 0.001) / r) / log(2);
    (nu == 1 ? s2 : nu == 2 ? s2 * 100 : r * pow(2, s2 / 12) - r) - base;
  );
);
// A frequency in Hz (Spread, Low cut, High cut, Tuning reference): Semitones and Cents move
// it by an interval, counted from the value -- or from `floor` when the value is below it.
// A Low cut at 0 is off, and an interval needs a pitch to count from; staying still there
// would not do as advertised (Rozaya: "I'd expect it to do as advertised lol ... And I'd
// promptly open an issue and file it as a bug report if it didn't"). Floors, both mine:
// 20 Hz for the cutoffs and the Tuning reference, the bottom of hearing; one FFT bin
// (srate / FFTSIZE, about 1.35 Hz at 44.1 kHz) for Spread -- measured 2026-09-12, Spread
// 1.0 and 1.3 render bit-identical to 0 and 1.4 does not. Either way the drift starts
// from nothing audible and climbs.
function au_freq(off, u, base, floor) local(b) (
  b = max(base, floor);
  u == 2 ? b * pow(2, off / 12) - base : u == 3 ? b * pow(2, off / 1200) - base : off;
);
// A length (Wash grain, the slot timings, Play for, Rest for) in nu {0 Seconds, 1 Hz,
// 2 Beats, 3 Milliseconds}; the first three are tm_sec's codes. Time units add seconds.
// Hz and BPM move the RATE the length is one cycle of, the way a timing in Hz already reads.
// A Cycle is one walk through the active slots' legs, as in the period units.
function au_sec(nu, v) ( nu == 3 ? v / 1000 : tm_sec(nu, v); );
function au_back(nu, t) ( nu == 3 ? t * 1000 : nu == 1 ? (t > 0.000001 ? 1 / t : 0) : nu == 2 ? t * max(tempo, 1) / 60 : t; );
function au_time(off, u, base, nu) local(b, t, rate) (
  !(u == 1 || (u >= 4 && u <= 9)) || (u == 4 && nu == 3) || (u == 5 && nu == 0) || (u == 1 && nu == 1) || (u == 8 && nu == 2) ? off : (
    b = au_sec(nu, base);
    u == 1 || u == 7 ? (
      rate = (b > 0.000001 ? 1 / b : 0) + (u == 7 ? off / 60 : off);
      t = rate > 0.000001 ? 1 / rate : 0;
    ) : (
      t = b + (u == 4 ? off / 1000 : u == 6 ? off * 60 : u == 8 ? off * 60 / max(tempo, 1) : u == 9 ? off * max(pv_cyc, 0.05) : off);
    );
    au_back(nu, max(0, t)) - base;
  );
);
// Drift + Ramp on one (slot, target) in the target's own unit, each amount converted in its
// own unit. kind 1 pitch value (nu = its unit), 2 frequency (nu = the floor in Hz),
// 3 length (nu = its unit); anything else takes them as they stand.
// Both pickers on Target default is exactly mo_q, so nothing saved changes.
function au_key(k, kind, base, nu) (
  (target_drift_unit[k] == 0 && ramp_by_unit[k] == 0) || kind < 1 || kind > 3 ? mo_q(k) :
  kind == 1 ? au_pitch(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_pitch(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu) :
  kind == 2 ? au_freq(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_freq(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu) :
              au_time(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, nu) + au_time(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, nu);
);
// Spread and its fine tune in a width unit {0 Hz, 1 Semitones, 2 Cents} (2026-09-16, the Morpher's):
// Semitones and Cents amounts convert into each other; anything else takes the amount as it stands.
function sp_amt(off, u, mode) ( u == 2 ? (mode == 2 ? off * 100 : off) : u == 3 ? (mode == 1 ? off / 100 : off) : off; );
function sp_q(k, mode) ( sp_amt(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], mode) + sp_amt(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], mode); );
// The cut blocks. A value in {0 Hz, 1 Semitones (a note number), 2 Cents} -> Hz.
function cut_hz_of(mode, v) ( mode == 0 ? v : mode == 1 ? tuning_ref * pow(2, (v - 69) / 12) : tuning_ref * pow(2, (v / 100 - 69) / 12); );
function cut_v_of(mode, hz) local(m) ( m = 69 + 12 * log(max(hz, 0.000001) / max(tuning_ref, 0.001)) / log(2); mode == 0 ? hz : mode == 1 ? m : m * 100; );
// Switching the mode keeps the frequency; 0 stays 0 (off).
function cut_conv(v, from, to) ( v <= 0 ? 0 : cut_v_of(to, cut_hz_of(from, v)); );
// The note name a value shows: 0 Off, else 1 + the nearest note number.
function cut_note(mode, v) local(m) (
  v <= 0 ? 0 : ( m = mode == 1 ? v : mode == 2 ? v / 100 : 69 + 12 * log(max(v, 0.000001) / max(tuning_ref, 0.001)) / log(2);
                 1 + max(0, min(127, floor(m + 0.5))); );
);
function cut_from_note(mode, n) ( n <= 0 ? 0 : mode == 0 ? tuning_ref * pow(2, (n - 1 - 69) / 12) : mode == 1 ? n - 1 : (n - 1) * 100; );
// A drift or ramp amount on a cut, in its mode's unit. Hz is au_freq, exactly as before.
function cut_amt(off, u, base, mode) local(f) (
  u == 1 ? ( f = cut_hz_of(mode, base) + off; (f > 0 ? cut_v_of(mode, f) : 0) - base; ) :
  u == 2 ? (mode == 2 ? off * 100 : off) : u == 3 ? (mode == 1 ? off / 100 : off) : off;
);
function cut_fine(hz, f, u) ( u == 0 ? hz + f : u == 1 ? hz * pow(2, f / 12) : hz * pow(2, f / 1200); );
function cut_q(k, base, mode, floor) (
  mode == 0 ? au_key(k, 2, base, floor) :
  cut_amt(target_drift_offset[k], target_drift_unit[k], base, mode) + cut_amt(ramp_offset_mem[k], ramp_by_unit[k], base, mode);
);
// One slot's heard cut in Hz (tgt: 6 Low cut, 7 High cut). Off (0) moves nothing. On, a drift stops
// at the cut's own frequency or `bottom`, whichever is lower, instead of switching it off.
function cut_slot_hz(sl, tgt, v, mode, funit, fine, bottom) local(cv, hz) (
  v <= 0 ? 0 : (
    cv = v + cut_q(sl*DSTRIDE + tgt, v, mode, 20);
    hz = mode == 0 ? cv : cut_hz_of(mode, cv);
    fine != 0 ? hz = cut_fine(hz, fine, funit);
    max(min(cut_hz_of(mode, v), bottom), min(20000, hz));
  );
);
// dmod with units: the morph pair's converted amounts, blended the way dmod blends them.
function dmodc(dmt, kind, bank, nu) (
  au_key(sb_i*DSTRIDE + dmt, kind, bank[sb_i], nu) * (1 - sb_f) + au_key(sb_j*DSTRIDE + dmt, kind, bank[sb_j], nu) * sb_f;
);
function ps_all(k, raw, v, bank) local(pa) (
  raw != ps_last[k] ? ( pa = 0; loop(NSLOTS, bank[pa] = v; pa += 1; ); ps_last[k] = raw; );
);
function ps_all_key(k, raw, tgt, bank) local(pa) (
  raw != ps_last[k] ? ( pa = 0; loop(NSLOTS, bank[pa*DSTRIDE + tgt] = raw; pa += 1; ); ps_last[k] = raw; );
);
function ps_adopt() (
  ps_last[0]  = slider3;  ps_last[1]  = slider4;  ps_last[2]  = slider47; ps_last[3]  = slider48;
  ps_last[4]  = slider12; ps_last[5]  = slider13; ps_last[6]  = slider14; ps_last[7]  = slider15;
  ps_last[8]  = slider18; ps_last[9]  = slider19; ps_last[10] = slider61; ps_last[11] = slider20;
  ps_last[12] = slider32; ps_last[13] = slider27;  ps_last[14] = slider35; ps_last[15] = slider39;
  ps_last[16] = slider36;
  ps_last[20] = slider70; ps_last[21] = slider71; ps_last[22] = slider73; ps_last[23] = slider75;
  ps_last[24] = slider82; ps_last[25] = slider84; ps_last[26] = slider89;
  ps_last[27] = slider76; ps_last[28] = slider77; ps_last[29] = slider85; ps_last[30] = slider86;
  ps_last[32] = slider22;  ps_last[33] = slider24;  ps_last[34] = slider23;  ps_last[35] = slider25;
  ps_last[36] = slider26; ps_last[37] = slider29; ps_last[38] = slider28;
  ps_last[39] = slider21; ps_last[40] = slider44; ps_last[41] = slider11;
  ps_last[42] = slider69; ps_last[43] = slider81; ps_last[31] = slider74;
  ps_last[44] = slider31; ps_last[45] = slider33; ps_last[46] = slider34; ps_last[47] = slider37; ps_last[48] = slider40;
  ps_last[49] = slider41; ps_last[50] = slider42; ps_last[51] = slider45; ps_last[52] = slider46;
  lc_last_mode = slider37; lc_last_note = slider38; lc_last_val = slider39;
  hc_last_mode = slider42; hc_last_note = slider43; hc_last_val = slider44;
  pv_last_tgt = slider25; pv_last_tval = slider27; pv_last_src = slider22; pv_last_sf = slider24; pv_last_sfu = slider23;
);
i = 0; loop(WTBL+1, sintab[i] = sin(TWOPI*i/WTBL); i += 1; );
// captured slots persist across transport play (one-time init)
slots_inited == 0 ? (
  slots_inited = 1;
  i = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_norm[i] = 1; slot_cappoint[i] = 0; slot_capavg[i] = 1; slot_ot_harm[i] = 0; slot_ot_depth[i] = 24; slot_linger[i] = 4; slot_xfade[i] = 1; slot_fadein[i] = 1; slot_gap[i] = 0; slot_xfadeon[i] = 1; slot_mute[i] = 0; slot_voicedb[i] = 0.0; slot_texture[i] = 0.5; slot_spread[i] = 0.0; slot_pitch[i] = 0.0; slot_width[i] = 50.0; slot_lowcut[i] = 0.0; slot_denoise[i] = 0.0; i += 1; );  // per-slot capture point defaults to 0 (earliest); @serialize restores/migrates
  i = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_tunit[i] = 1; slot_fine[i] = 0; slot_funit[i] = 2; slot_srcnote[i] = 0; slot_srcfine[i] = 0; slot_srcfunit[i] = 2; slot_tgtnote[i] = 60; i += 1; );  // pitch block: Semitones, no fine tune, Source note None
  i = 0; loop(NSLOTS, slot_grain[i] = 150; slot_hicut[i] = 0; slot_tmunit[i] = 0;
  slot_spmode[i] = 0; slot_spfunit[i] = 2; slot_spfine[i] = 0; slot_lcmode[i] = 0; slot_lcfunit[i] = 2; slot_lcfine[i] = 0;
  slot_hcmode[i] = 0; slot_hcfunit[i] = 2; slot_hcfine[i] = 0; i += 1; );   // Wash grain's default; cuts off
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, doff[i] = rand(1)*2 - 1; i += 1; );
  // have / n_used live in this ONE-TIME guard so captures survive transport
  // play (@init re-runs on every play; if these were reset there, a capture
  // made in one play would be orphaned on the next).
  have = 0; n_used = 0;
  // Drift CONFIG defaults live in the same one-time guard, for the same reason:
  // they must survive transport play. @serialize overwrites these with the
  // saved config on project load (the guard runs on fresh load, BEFORE the
  // @serialize restore — the exact order the captures above already rely on).
  i = 0; loop(DBANK,
    target_drift_up[i]    = 0;
    target_drift_down[i]  = 0;
    target_drift_per[i]   = 30;
    target_drift_shape[i] = 0;
    ramp_by_mem[i]    = 0;   // Ramp CONFIG defaults (same one-time-guard rationale)
    ramp_dur_mem[i]   = 0;   // 0 duration = ramp inert until the user sets it
    ramp_delay_mem[i] = 0;
    target_drift_unit[i] = 0; ramp_by_unit[i] = 0;   // Target default
    target_drift_moves[i] = (i % DSTRIDE) >= 10 && (i % DSTRIDE) <= 13 ? 0 : 1;   // slot timings with the target
    i += 1;
  );
  last_drift_key = 0;   // composite (slot, target) keys now, not bare targets
  last_ramp_key  = 0;
  last_cap_slot      = 0;   // per-slot Capture-point selector (mirrors the Drift/Ramp target selectors)
  last_cap_sel = 1; last_cap_all = 0; cap_all_scrub = 0;   // All slots: 0 = All, 1-8 = Slot 1-8
);
// Drift RUNTIME state (phase/prev/curr/offset) is reset here on load AND on the
// transport play-edge in @block, so drift restarts at phase 0 on every play
// (continues across stop) — matching the rest of the suite.
i = 0; loop(DBANK,
  target_drift_phase[i] = 0; target_drift_prev[i] = 0;
  target_drift_curr[i]  = 0; target_drift_offset[i] = 0;
  ramp_t_mem[i] = 0; ramp_delay_elapsed_mem[i] = 0; ramp_offset_mem[i] = 0;
  i += 1;
);
eff_spread_hz = 0; eff_lowcut_hz = 0; eff_stereo_width = 0; sp_mode = 0; sp_semis = 0; sp_extra_hz = 0;   // seeded properly in @slider/@sample
// runtime state
cap_last = 0; first_slider = 1; inpos = 0; last_gsamp = -1; last_cappoint = 0; last_capavg = 1;
cap_dirty = 0; settle = 0;   // debounce for Capture-point re-analysis
// Captured spectra re-derive LAZILY -- only when actually stale (a fresh project
// load, or a capture-point scrub), never on an ordinary stop/play. In the normal
// capture-then-play flow nothing re-derives at all. Starts stale on load
// (@serialize restores the raw audio but not the analysis derived from it).
analysis_valid = 0;
rms_smooth = 0;   // slow auto-gain state (stable loudness, no per-grain pump)
cap_point01 = 0;  // analysis position in the grab (slider3); 0 = earliest
texture = 0.5; hlevel = 0.707; wlevel = 0.707;   // voice<->wash crossfade
i = 0; loop(NHARM, hph[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; hphB[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; hphR[i] = hph[i]; hphBR[i] = hphB[i]; i += 1; );
// The wavetable voice (2026-09-16) starts from those same phases: bank 0 is A's, bank 1 is B's.
i = 0; loop(NHARM, wt_phi[i] = hph[i]; wt_phi[NHARM + i] = hphB[i]; i += 1; );
i = 0; loop(2, wt_ph[i*2] = 0; wt_ph[i*2+1] = 0; wt_act[i] = 0; wt_xf[i] = 0; wt_wait[i] = 0;
               wt_bank[i] = i; wt_slot[i] = -1; i += 1; );
voice_detune = 0;   // right-channel voice detune amount (from Stereo width)
// Overtone: gains start flat (feature off), and the smoothers start settled so
// the first sample does not glide up from nowhere. ~30 ms takes the step out of
// a manual move of the harmonic or the depth -- both are gain changes across
// tens of partials at once, which steps the waveform if applied raw.
i = 0; loop(NHARM, ot_gain[i] = 1; i += 1; );
ot_coeff = 1 - exp(-1 / (0.03 * srate));
ot_harm_base = 0; ot_depth_db = 24; ot_width = 1;
ot_h_smooth = 0; ot_db_smooth = 24; ot_w_smooth = 1;
ot_on_last = 0; ot_h_last = -1; ot_db_last = -1; ot_w_last = -1;
ot_boost = 1; ot_norm = 1; ot_bin = 0; ot_binw = 1; ot_f0bin = 0;   // wash-side, until @sample sets them
i = 0; loop(WA, window[i] = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*i/(WA-1)); i += 1; );   // analysis window (fixed)
morph_phase = 0; eff_morph01 = 0; last_automode = -1; last_ps = 0;
drift_was_playing = 0;   // Drift restart-on-play edge state
cur_slot = 0; nxt_slot = 0; xfade = 0;             // Drift: direct crossfade cur_slot -> nxt_slot
// Previous sample's morph pair, for the voice phase handover in @sample. -1 so
// the very first sample records the pair without copying anything (there is no
// previous waveform to stay continuous with).
last_vsi = -1; last_vsj = -1;
bag_pos = 999; last_slot = 0; drift_primed = 0; auto_primed = 0; sweep_dir = 1; leg_elapsed = 0;   // walk + shuffle-bag state (primed lazily in @sample)
// Half-2 leg envelope: leg_env is the per-sample output level (0..1) that makes
// fade-in / fade-out / gap silence; need_fadein tracks whether the current leg
// began from silence (fade it in) or arrived at full via a crossfade (don't).
leg_env = 1; need_fadein = 1; out_env = 1;
// Slot-timing drift/ramp offsets, SAMPLED once per leg (see the leg machine).
lgo_fi = 0; lgo_h = 0; lgo_fo = 0; lgo_g = 0;
fade_in_shape = 1; fade_out_shape = 1;   // Cosine until @slider reads sliders 33/34
// Fresh instance: every slot starts as whatever is on screen. Without this only
// the slot selected when a slider was last touched would carry the visible
// settings, and the others would sit at hard-coded defaults.
seed_slots_from_sliders = 1;
// Scramble the RNG per load: EEL rand() otherwise replays the SAME sequence
// every session, so Drift would wander the identical path each time you open the
// project. Advance it a sub-second-time-dependent number of steps so each load
// starts somewhere different. One-time, bounded, ~free.
_tp = time_precise(); i = 0; loop(floor(1 + (_tp - floor(_tp)) * 500), rand(1); i += 1; );

// wavetable sine: linear-interpolated lookup of one precomputed period. Only the
// sin() EVALUATION changes; phase accumulators/increments are untouched, so every
// harmonic stays exactly at (n+1)*f0 -- no detune, no inharmonic content. Phase
// accumulators wrap to [0, TWOPI), so fidx lands in [0, WTBL) and the guard sample
// at sintab[WTBL] covers i0 = WTBL-1.
function wtsin(ph) local(fidx, i0, fr) (
  fidx = ph * WTSCALE;
  i0 = floor(fidx); fr = fidx - i0;
  sintab[i0]*(1-fr) + sintab[i0+1]*fr;
);

// grab the most recent MAXFFT input samples into a slot's raw store
function grab_raw(slot) local(i, idx, base) (
  base = slot*MAXFFT;
  i = 0;
  loop(MAXFFT, idx = inpos - MAXFFT + i; idx < 0 ? idx += INRING; slotraw[base+i] = inbuf[idx]; i += 1; );
);

// FFT WA samples of a slot's raw, Hann-windowed, zero-padded to FFTSIZE. Uses the
// FIXED analysis window WA (not the synthesis grain W), so Wash grain never
// triggers this. Capture point picks WHERE in the ~0.68 s grab to look: 0 =
// earliest (the sustained vowel, since you react a beat after hearing it) .. 1 =
// the press-moment.
// Averages the MAGNITUDES of `Capture average` frames stepped through the grab
// by CAPAVG_HOP, centred on the capture point.
//
// At 1 frame this is the original analysis exactly (the loop runs once, the
// centring term is zero, the divide is by one), so nothing that already exists
// changes until the dial is turned.
//
// Above 1 it fixes the wobble on re-spectralised material. One frame freezes
// that frame's per-bin scatter, and the wash replays the frozen magnitudes on
// every single grain -- so scatter that would average out over time instead
// recurs, identically, hundreds of times a second. On natural voice successive
// frames look alike and the frozen frame is a fair description of the sound; on
// material that has already been through a spectral process the scatter is
// irregular and its repetition IS the wobble. Averaging lets the scatter cancel
// while the spectral shape, which is what the frames agree on, survives.
//
// The frames are centred on the capture point and then clamped inside the grab,
// so the capture point still chooses which moment is described -- it just
// describes it with a short span rather than an instant. What you trade is
// time-smear: a vowel that MOVES gets blended across the span, which is why
// this is a dial rather than a default.
function compute_spectrum(slot) local(i, f, re, im, mx, rbase, mbase, kk, lo, hi, fstart, fb) (
  kk = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, floor(slot_capavg[slot])));
  lo = slot*MAXFFT;
  rbase = lo + floor(slot_cappoint[slot] * (MAXFFT - WA));   // per-slot capture point
  // Last first-frame start that still leaves room for all kk frames.
  hi = lo + MAXFFT - WA - (kk-1)*CAPAVG_HOP;
  fstart = rbase - floor((kk-1)/2)*CAPAVG_HOP;               // centre the span on the point
  fstart < lo ? fstart = lo;
  fstart > hi ? fstart = hi;
  mbase = slot*NBINS;
  i = 0; loop(NBINS, slotmag[mbase+i] = 0; i += 1; );
  f = 0;
  loop(kk,
    fb = fstart + f*CAPAVG_HOP;
    i = 0; loop(WA, fftwork[2*i] = slotraw[fb+i]*window[i]; fftwork[2*i+1] = 0; i += 1; );
    i = WA; loop(FFTSIZE - WA, fftwork[2*i] = 0; fftwork[2*i+1] = 0; i += 1; );
    fft(fftwork, FFTSIZE); fft_permute(fftwork, FFTSIZE);
    // Sum MAGNITUDES, not complex bins. Summing complex would let frames cancel
    // each other by phase -- which is a comb filter, not an average.
    i = 0;
    loop(NBINS,
      re = fftwork[2*i]; im = fftwork[2*i+1];
      slotmag[mbase+i] += sqrt(re*re + im*im);
      i += 1;
    );
    f += 1;
  );
  mx = 0; i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    slotmag[mbase+i] = slotmag[mbase+i] / kk;
    slotmag[mbase+i] > mx ? mx = slotmag[mbase+i];
    i += 1;
  );
  slot_norm[slot] = mx > 0 ? mx : 1;
);

// YIN-lite pitch detect (+ subharmonic octave fix) then 64 harmonic magnitudes
// (direct DFT). VOICE end of the Texture knob -- harmonics at the exact detected
// f0 avoid the bin-drift that wrecks phase-coherent resynth from a frozen frame.
function analyze_harm(slot) local(base, ci, almin, almax, tmax, tau, ii, d, dd, run, thr, res, cand, lockon, best, pa, pb, pc, pden, psh, f0d, hn, hf, kk, re, im, ang, hannv, mag, mx, boff) (
  base = slot*MAXFFT + floor(slot_cappoint[slot] * (MAXFFT - WHARM));   // same per-slot point as the wash
  ci = 0; loop(WHARM, anbuf[ci] = slotraw[base+ci]; ci += 1; );
  almin = floor(srate/600); almax = ceil(srate/70); tmax = min(2*almax, WHARM-1);
  tau = 1;
  loop(tmax, d = 0; ii = 0; loop(WHARM - tmax, dd = anbuf[ii] - anbuf[ii+tau]; d += dd*dd; ii += 1; ); yin_df[tau] = d; tau += 1; );
  yin_cmnd[0] = 1; run = 0; tau = 1;
  loop(tmax, run += yin_df[tau]; yin_cmnd[tau] = yin_df[tau]*tau/(run+0.000001); tau += 1; );
  thr = 0.15; res = -1; cand = -1; lockon = 0; tau = almin;
  loop(almax - almin,
    res < 0 ? ( lockon == 0 ? ( yin_cmnd[tau] < thr ? ( lockon = 1; cand = tau; ); )
                            : ( yin_cmnd[tau] < yin_cmnd[cand] ? ( cand = tau; ) : ( res = cand; ); ); );
    tau += 1;
  );
  (res < 0 && lockon == 1) ? res = cand;
  res < 0 ? ( best = 1000000000; tau = almin; loop(almax - almin, yin_cmnd[tau] < best ? ( best = yin_cmnd[tau]; res = tau; ); tau += 1; ); );
  loop(3, (2*res <= tmax && yin_cmnd[2*res] < 0.25 && yin_cmnd[2*res] < 1.15*yin_cmnd[res]) ? res = 2*res; );
  (res > 1 && res < tmax) ? ( pa = yin_cmnd[res-1]; pb = yin_cmnd[res]; pc = yin_cmnd[res+1]; pden = pa-2*pb+pc; psh = (pden!=0)?0.5*(pa-pc)/pden:0; ) : ( psh = 0; );
  f0d = srate/(res+psh);
  (f0d > 20 && f0d < srate*0.5) ? (
    boff = slot*NHARM; mx = 0; hn = 1;
    loop(NHARM,
      hf = hn*f0d;
      hf < srate*0.5 ? (
        re = 0; im = 0; kk = 0;
        loop(WHARM, hannv = 0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*kk/(WHARM-1)); ang = TWOPI*hf*kk/srate; re += anbuf[kk]*hannv*cos(ang); im -= anbuf[kk]*hannv*sin(ang); kk += 1; );
        mag = sqrt(re*re + im*im);
      ) : ( mag = 0; );
      slot_harm[boff + (hn-1)] = mag; mag > mx ? mx = mag; hn += 1;
    );
    slot_f0[slot] = f0d; slot_hnorm[slot] = mx > 0 ? mx : 1; harm_ver += 1;
  );
);

function capture(slot) (
  n_used == 0 ? analysis_valid = 1;   // the first capture: nothing else is waiting to be analysed
  grab_raw(slot);
  slot_cappoint[slot] = cap_point01;   // stamp this slot with the current capture point; scrubbing later re-tunes only this slot
  slot_capavg[slot]   = cap_avg;       // and with the frame count on screen, so the capture is analyzed the way you were listening
  slot_ot_harm[slot]  = max(0, min(NHARM, slider47));   // and the overtone it was sung with
  slot_ot_depth[slot] = max(0, min(48, slider48));
  compute_spectrum(slot);   // wash (spectral) analysis
  analyze_harm(slot);       // voice (harmonic) analysis -- same captured frame
  have = 1;
  (slot + 1) > n_used ? n_used = slot + 1;
);

function build_spectrum(useR) local(i, srcb, b0, fr, m, ph, woff, re, im, mir) (
  woff = eff_stereo_width * 0.01 * PI;   // eff_ = base + Drift offset (set per-sample)
  // Build a Hermitian-symmetric spectrum so ifft() returns a REAL signal:
  // fill bins 0..N/2 from the magnitude data, mirror them as conjugates.
  i = 0;
  loop(GBINS,
    // GR maps this grain's bin grid back onto the fixed analysis grid, so the
    // same frequency is read whatever size the synthesis FFT currently is.
    // No pitch term: transposition is applied per slot in gen_grain, before the
    // two spectra are blended. GR only maps grain grid -> analysis grid.
    srcb = i * GR;
    (srcb >= 0 && srcb < NBINS - 1) ? ( b0 = floor(srcb); fr = srcb - b0; m = (curmag[b0]*(1-fr) + curmag[b0+1]*fr) * GINV; ) : ( m = 0; );
    // High cut, after each slot's transposition: i is an absolute output frequency.
    hc_on ? ( i >= hcg_hi ? ( m = 0; ) : ( i > hcg_lo ? ( m *= (hcg_hi - i) * hcg_inv; ); ); );
    ph = phase[i] + (useR ? woff*doff[i] : 0);
    re = m * cos(ph); im = m * sin(ph);
    fftwork[2*i] = re; fftwork[2*i+1] = im;
    (i > 0 && i < GFFT/2) ? ( mir = GFFT - i; fftwork[2*mir] = re; fftwork[2*mir+1] = -im; );
    i += 1;
  );
  fftwork[1] = 0;                       // DC imaginary = 0
  fftwork[2*(GFFT/2)+1] = 0;            // Nyquist imaginary = 0
  fft_ipermute(fftwork, GFFT);
  ifft(fftwork, GFFT);
);

function gen_grain() local(i, j, si, sj, mfr, ni, nj, bi, bj, sp, sumv, cnt, bj2, pk, thr, cutbin, sv, rms, gnorm, aw,
                          sA, sB, ia, ib, fa, fb, ma, mb, npc, pk2, pst, pn, pw) (
  // slot selection (vsi/vsj/vmfr) is computed once per sample in @sample so the
  // wash and the harmonic voice always agree on which slot(s) they're playing.
  si = vsi; sj = vsj; mfr = vmfr;
  ni = slot_norm[si]; ni <= 0 ? ni = 1;
  nj = slot_norm[sj]; nj <= 0 ? nj = 1;
  bi = si*NBINS; bj = sj*NBINS;
  // --- Pitch is applied HERE, per slot, BEFORE the blend. ---
  // It used to be applied in build_spectrum, i.e. AFTER these two spectra were
  // averaged together -- which meant one shared pitch dragged across the whole
  // blend, so a crossfade between two differently-tuned slots slid from one
  // tuning to the other over the fade. That's a glide, not a crossfade.
  //
  // Transposition here is only a bin-index remap on read (read bin i/ratio to
  // sound bin i), so doing it per slot costs one interpolation per bin rather
  // than a second grain and a second iFFT -- and build_spectrum drops a divide
  // per bin in exchange. Each slot is now read at its OWN tuning and the blend
  // mixes two correctly-pitched spectra, so the morph fades in level only.
  //
  // Reads past the top of the analysis grid (pitching down far enough that
  // i/ratio runs off the end) contribute silence, the same guard
  // build_spectrum used to apply.
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    sA = i / pitch_ratio_A;
    sB = i / pitch_ratio_B;
    (sA >= 0 && sA < NBINS - 1) ? (
      ia = floor(sA); fa = sA - ia;
      ma = (slotmag[bi+ia]*(1-fa) + slotmag[bi+ia+1]*fa) / ni;
    ) : ( ma = 0; );
    (sB >= 0 && sB < NBINS - 1) ? (
      ib = floor(sB); fb = sB - ib;
      mb = (slotmag[bj+ib]*(1-fb) + slotmag[bj+ib+1]*fb) / nj;
    ) : ( mb = 0; );
    mtmp[i] = (1-mfr)*ma + mfr*mb;
    i += 1;
  );
  sp = sp_mode == 0 ? floor(eff_spread_hz * FFTSIZE / srate) : 0;   // eff_ = base + Drift offset
  // Spread is a box blur across frequency bins. It used to re-add every
  // neighbour from scratch for each bin, so its cost grew with the width -- at
  // Spread 100 Hz that is more work than both iFFTs of the grain combined, and
  // it was the single most expensive thing in a wash-heavy project.
  //
  // Same result, computed as a RUNNING TOTAL: entering bin i, add the neighbour
  // that came into range and subtract the one that left. Cost no longer depends
  // on the width at all -- Spread 150 costs what Spread 1 costs. The window is
  // clipped at both ends and divided by its real width, exactly as the counted
  // version did, so edge bins are averaged over the same neighbours as before.
  i = 0;
  sp > 0 ? (
    // seed the window for bin 0: [0 .. sp]
    sumv = 0; cnt = 0; j = 0;
    loop(min(sp, NBINS - 1) + 1, sumv += mtmp[j]; cnt += 1; j += 1; );
  );
  loop(NBINS,
    sp <= 0 ? ( curmag[i] = mtmp[i]; ) : (
      curmag[i] = sumv / cnt;
      // slide to bin i+1: gain (i+1+sp) if it exists, drop (i-sp) if it existed
      bj2 = i + 1 + sp; bj2 < NBINS ? ( sumv += mtmp[bj2]; cnt += 1; );
      bj2 = i - sp;     bj2 >= 0    ? ( sumv -= mtmp[bj2]; cnt -= 1; );
    );
    i += 1;
  );
  // Spread in Semitones or Cents (2026-09-16, the Morpher's): each bin blurs from f / r to f * r,
  // r = 2^(width/12), plus any fine tune in Hz on both sides -- a window that grows with frequency,
  // so running sums (spc) give each bin its own mean in one pass.
  sp_mode != 0 && (sp_semis > 0 || sp_extra_hz > 0) ? (
    sp_r = pow(2, sp_semis / 12); sp_ex = sp_extra_hz * FFTSIZE / srate;
    spc[0] = 0; i = 0; loop(NBINS, spc[i + 1] = spc[i] + mtmp[i]; i += 1; );
    i = 0;
    loop(NBINS,
      sp_lo = max(0, min(i, floor(i / sp_r - sp_ex + 0.5)));
      sp_hi = min(NBINS - 1, max(i, floor(i * sp_r + sp_ex + 0.5)));
      curmag[i] = (spc[sp_hi + 1] - spc[sp_lo]) / (sp_hi - sp_lo + 1);
      i += 1;
    );
  );
  pk = 0; i = 0; loop(NBINS, curmag[i] > pk ? pk = curmag[i]; i += 1; );
  thr = eff_denoise * 0.01 * 0.1 * pk;
  cutbin = floor(eff_lowcut_hz * FFTSIZE / srate);   // eff_ = base + Drift offset
  // High cut in the GRAIN's output bins, read at grain time (a grain is a snapshot).
  hcg_hi  = hc_on ? hc_top * GFFT / srate : GBINS + 1;
  hcg_lo  = hc_on ? hc_lo  * GFFT / srate : GBINS + 1;
  hcg_inv = hc_on ? 1 / max(1, hcg_hi - hcg_lo) : 0;
  // Overtone on the WASH. Same control, same window shape, expressed in bins
  // instead of partials. The wash keeps the magnitude spectrum -- the harmonic
  // peaks are still there, phase-randomised into narrow noise bands -- so
  // isolating one leaves a pitched band rather than nothing. Breathier than the
  // voice engine's pure partial, which is what Texture means everywhere else in
  // this plugin: same gesture, different material.
  //
  // ot_bin / ot_binw / ot_boost / ot_norm / ot_f0bin are set per sample in @sample, off the
  // SAME smoothed controls the voice uses, so the two engines stay in step
  // through a Texture blend rather than describing different overtones.
  ot_wash = (ot_on && ot_bin > 0) ? 1 : 0;
  i = 0;
  loop(NBINS,
    i < cutbin ? ( curmag[i] = 0; ) : ( curmag[i] = max(0, curmag[i] - thr); );
    ot_wash ? (
      // Bins under the fundamental's own peak are never lifted, mirroring harmonic
      // 1 in the voice: it is the drone the overtone sings over. They still take
      // ot_norm, so the whole wash recedes together rather than the band alone
      // rising out of an untouched bed.
      i > ot_f0bin ? (
        od = abs(i - ot_bin) / ot_binw;
        ow = od >= 1 ? 0 : (0.5 + 0.5*wtsin(od*PI + PI*0.5));
        curmag[i] = curmag[i] * (1 + (ot_boost - 1)*ow) * ot_norm;
      ) : (
        curmag[i] = curmag[i] * ot_norm;
      );
    );
    i += 1;
  );
  W <= FFTSIZE ? (
  // --- A grain that fits one FFTSIZE piece: the original build, unchanged. ---
  // Only the bins this grain's FFT actually reads need a fresh random phase.
  // Was NBINS unconditionally -- another cost that ignored grain size.
  i = 0; loop(GBINS, phase[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; i += 1; );
  build_spectrum(0);
  rms = 0; i = 0; loop(W, sv = fftwork[2*i]; grainbuf[i] = sv; rms += sv*sv; i += 1; );
  rms = sqrt(rms / W);
  // Slow auto-gain: smooth the grain RMS across grains so loudness is stable and
  // does NOT pump per grain. With the sqrt-Hann constant-power window below,
  // overlapping random-phase grains sum to steady power. Target ~0.07 grain RMS
  // -> ~0.1 output RMS (-20 dBFS). Freeze the smoother on silent grains (empty
  // slot) so switching back doesn't blip loud.
  rms > 0.0000001 ? ( rms_smooth <= 0 ? rms_smooth = rms : rms_smooth += 0.02 * (rms - rms_smooth); );
  gnorm = rms_smooth > 0.0000001 ? (0.07 / rms_smooth) : 0;
  build_spectrum(1);
  i = 0;
  loop(W,
    aw = accpos + i; aw >= ACCLEN ? aw -= ACCLEN;
    accL[aw] += grainbuf[i]   * gnorm * synwin[i];
    accR[aw] += fftwork[2*i]  * gnorm * synwin[i];
    i += 1;
  );
  ) : (
  // --- A grain longer than FFTSIZE (2026-09-13), the Morpher's build: FFTSIZE pieces every
  //     half piece, each with fresh random phases, summed under pwin. The first piece is
  //     not faded in and the last not faded out -- synwin shapes the grain's two ends.
  //     build_synwin has already held GFFT at FFTSIZE for any grain this long. ---
  npc = ceil((W - FFTSIZE) / (FFTSIZE/2)) + 1;
  i = 0; loop(W, grainbuf[i] = 0; grainR[i] = 0; i += 1; );
  pk2 = 0;
  loop(npc,
    pst = pk2 * FFTSIZE/2;
    pn = min(FFTSIZE, W - pst);
    i = 0; loop(GBINS, phase[i] = rand(1)*TWOPI; i += 1; );
    build_spectrum(0);
    j = 0;
    loop(pn,
      pw = ((pk2 == 0 && j < FFTSIZE/2) || (pk2 == npc - 1 && j >= FFTSIZE/2)) ? 1 : pwin[j];
      grainbuf[pst + j] += fftwork[2*j] * pw;
      j += 1;
    );
    build_spectrum(1);
    j = 0;
    loop(pn,
      pw = ((pk2 == 0 && j < FFTSIZE/2) || (pk2 == npc - 1 && j >= FFTSIZE/2)) ? 1 : pwin[j];
      grainR[pst + j] += fftwork[2*j] * pw;
      j += 1;
    );
    pk2 += 1;
  );
  rms = 0; i = 0; loop(W, sv = grainbuf[i]; rms += sv*sv; i += 1; );
  rms = sqrt(rms / W);
  rms > 0.0000001 ? ( rms_smooth <= 0 ? rms_smooth = rms : rms_smooth += 0.02 * (rms - rms_smooth); );
  gnorm = rms_smooth > 0.0000001 ? (0.07 / rms_smooth) : 0;
  i = 0;
  loop(W,
    aw = accpos + i; aw >= ACCLEN ? aw -= ACCLEN;
    accL[aw] += grainbuf[i] * gnorm * synwin[i];
    accR[aw] += grainR[i]   * gnorm * synwin[i];
    i += 1;
  );
  );
);

// Rebuild ONLY the synthesis grain window (sqrt-Hann, length W = Wash grain).
// CHEAP: no FFT, no YIN, no accumulator clear -- so Wash grain can be dragged
// or AUTOMATED without dropouts (old-length grains finish as new ones start).
// GFFT: the synthesis FFT is sized TO THE GRAIN, not fixed at FFTSIZE.
//
// A grain of W samples cannot carry more spectral detail than W supports, so
// synthesizing FFTSIZE points and then keeping only the first W was buying
// nothing -- it was pure discarded work, and the shorter the grain the more of
// it got thrown away. Worse, cost-per-grain was CONSTANT while grains fire
// every W/4 samples, so shortening the grain multiplied CPU linearly: at the
// bottom of the Wash grain range the wash fired ~800 grains/sec, each paying
// two full 32768-point iFFTs. That is the "even dropouts at short grain"
// symptom -- real-time underrun, not a windowing artifact.
//
// Sizing the FFT to the grain inverts that: short grains become the CHEAPEST
// setting instead of the most expensive, and long grains are unchanged in
// character (at the default grain the old FFT was already longer than the
// grain itself, so the extra resolution was never audible).
//
// GR is the source->target bin stride. The captured spectra stay on the fixed
// FFTSIZE analysis grid; only synthesis resolution adapts.
// Crossfade one per-slot value the same way the spectra are crossfaded.
function sblend(arr, i, j, f) ( arr[i] * (1 - f) + arr[j] * f );

// Drift + Ramp offset for one target, BLENDED between the two morphing slots by
// the same fraction that blends the values it modifies (sb_i / sb_j / sb_f are
// set in @sample just before the effective values are computed).
//
// The blend is not cosmetic. Per-slot offsets read raw would STEP at every slot
// handoff -- slot A's wander replaced by slot B's in one sample, on a value
// feeding Texture or Low cut. That is a discontinuity in precisely the place the
// voice phase handover exists to keep continuous, and it would click for the
// same reason. Blending it means the modulation crossfades with everything else.
function dmod(dmt) (
    (target_drift_offset[sb_i*DSTRIDE + dmt] + ramp_offset_mem[sb_i*DSTRIDE + dmt]) * (1 - sb_f)
  + (target_drift_offset[sb_j*DSTRIDE + dmt] + ramp_offset_mem[sb_j*DSTRIDE + dmt]) * sb_f
);

function build_synwin(wlen) local(i) (
  wlen = max(64, min(GMAX, floor(wlen)));   // was MAXFFT: see GMAX
  W = wlen; HOP = max(1, floor(W / 4));
  GFFT = 256;
  loop(16, GFFT < W ? GFFT *= 2; );      // bounded: 256 << 16 covers MAXFFT
  GFFT = min(GFFT, FFTSIZE);
  GBINS = GFFT/2 + 1;
  GR = FFTSIZE / GFFT;                   // integer: both are powers of two
  // JSFX ifft() is UNNORMALIZED ("scaled down by 1/size, if used"), so raw grain
  // amplitude tracks GFFT. Folding 1/GFFT into the magnitudes keeps grain level
  // independent of grain size -- without it the slow auto-gain smoother would
  // have to chase a large jump every time GFFT crossed a power of two, i.e.
  // dragging Wash grain would swell and dip. Free: it rides an existing multiply.
  GINV = 1 / GFFT;
  i = 0; loop(W, synwin[i] = sqrt(0.5 - 0.5*cos(TWOPI*i/(W-1))); i += 1; );
);
function clear_accum() local(i) (
  i = 0; loop(ACCLEN, accL[i] = 0; accR[i] = 0; i += 1; );
  accpos = 0; hopcount = 0;
);
// Empty ONLY the audio still waiting to be read, leaving the read pointer and
// the grain cadence alone. Same job as clear_accum -- the sequential-handoff
// flush that stops the previous slot's tail bleeding into the next one -- but
// affordable to call from @sample.
//
// A grain is written W samples ahead of wherever the read pointer stood, and the
// pointer only ever moves forward, so everything unread necessarily lives inside
// [accpos, accpos + W). Clearing that window empties the buffer just as
// completely as wiping all ACCLEN slots did; the other ~58000 slots wiped by the
// full version were already-read zeros. At the default grain that is ~7200
// samples instead of 65536, and it shrinks further as the grain shortens.
//
// This matters because the caller is in @sample: the full wipe was 131072 writes
// crammed into a SINGLE sample, a spike large enough to cost a whole block on a
// loaded project. accpos / hopcount are deliberately NOT reset here -- moving
// the read pointer would walk it straight into slots this function never
// cleared, which is the one way to reintroduce the bleed it exists to prevent.
function flush_accum_pending() local(i, aw) (
  i = 0;
  loop(W,
    aw = accpos + i; aw >= ACCLEN ? aw -= ACCLEN;
    accL[aw] = 0; accR[aw] = 0;
    i += 1;
  );
);
// Fade-curve shaping -- shared math with the sweeping-filter suite's apply_curve.
// Maps a rising 0..1 ramp to a shaped rising 0..1: the leg fade-in uses
// apply_curve(t, fade_in_shape) and the fade-out uses 1 - apply_curve(t,
// fade_out_shape). Default (Cosine, 1) is the smooth eased S the suite defaults
// to everywhere; Linear (0) is the old straight ramp.
function apply_curve(t, shape) (
  shape == 0 ? t :
  shape == 1 ? (0.5 - 0.5 * cos(t * $pi)) :
  shape == 2 ? (log(1 + 9*t) / log(10)) :
               ((10^t - 1) / 9)
);

// Fisher-Yates shuffle of the first n_used slot indices into driftbag. Drift
// draws its next slot from this bag, so it visits every captured slot exactly
// once per cycle (even coverage, random order) rather than a memoryless walk
// that clusters in the middle. Avoids an immediate repeat across the reshuffle
// seam (if the new first slot equals the one just visited, swap it back one).
// Recompute the list of active (unmuted) slot indices. Called every @block
// (cheap: max 8 iterations) and after any capture, so mute toggles land
// immediately. n_active is the count; active_slots[0..n_active-1] are the
// indices. When 0 slots are active, morph goes silent; when 1, it plays that
// slot solo; when 2+, the sequencer walks the active list.
function rebuild_active_slots() local(i) (
  n_active = 0;
  i = 0;
  loop(n_used,
    slot_mute[i] < 0.5 ? (
      active_slots[n_active] = i;
      n_active += 1;
    );
    i += 1;
  );
);

// Position of `slot` in the active list, or -1 if not present. Used by Sweep
// to find where cur_slot sits so it can walk to the next active neighbour.
function active_pos(slot) local(i, pos) (
  pos = -1;
  i = 0;
  loop(n_active, active_slots[i] == slot ? pos = i; i += 1; );
  pos;
);

function shuffle_bag() local(i, j, t) (
  // Shuffle only the ACTIVE (unmuted) slot indices, not raw 0..n_used.
  i = 0; loop(n_active, driftbag[i] = active_slots[i]; i += 1; );
  i = n_active - 1;
  loop(n_active - 1,
    j = floor(rand(i + 1));
    t = driftbag[i]; driftbag[i] = driftbag[j]; driftbag[j] = t;
    i -= 1;
  );
  (n_active > 1 && driftbag[0] == last_slot) ? ( t = driftbag[0]; driftbag[0] = driftbag[1]; driftbag[1] = t; );
);

// Seed the grain window and its FFT size here rather than relying on @block
// reaching the "grain length changed" branch first. In practice it always does
// (last_gsamp starts at 0), but GFFT / GBINS / GR / GINV are now divisors and
// loop counts in the synthesis path -- leaving them at 0 for even one block
// would be a divide-by-zero, so the dependency is made explicit instead of
// inherited from initialization order.
build_synwin(max(64, floor(150 * srate / 1000)));

@slider
// Every slider read into a named global here; nothing downstream references a
// sliderN directly, so the layout can be reordered without desyncing the engine.
// All slots: index 0 is All, 1-8 are the slots (the migration moved saved values up one).
cap_sel       = max(0, min(NSLOTS, floor(slider1 + 0.5)));
cap_all       = cap_sel == 0;
cap_slot      = cap_all ? 0 : cap_sel - 1;   // engine is 0-7; All shows and auditions Slot 1
// Keep the flag's value: the slot-switch test below needs to know this is the
// first @slider after a load, and the line that consumes it runs before that.
is_first_slider = first_slider;
cap_trig      = slider2; first_slider ? ( cap_last = cap_trig; first_slider = 0; );  // first @slider after load: sync cap_last so a saved-ON Capture-now never fires a silent capture on reload
// --- Per-slot Capture point (nested-selector, like Drift/Ramp targets). The
//     Capture-point slider is now PER SLOT: switching Capture slot saves the
//     old slot's point and loads the new slot's stored point into slider3, so
//     each slot keeps its own by-ear tuning. Editing slider3 writes the current
//     slot. Suppress the @block re-derive on a pure slot-switch (each slot is
//     already analyzed at its own point) by syncing last_cappoint. ---
tuning_base = slider30 >= 20 ? slider30 : 440; tuning_ref = tuning_base;
// Source note and Target note mirror Transpose value -- Bubbler's design -- only while
// a Source note is set and Transpose is in Semitones. Reconciled BEFORE the slot banks
// capture, so the sound never lags the display. Source note and its fine tune never
// change the sound: moving them re-reads the Target note. Picking a Target note writes
// the shift. Trackers are refreshed after the slot block below and in ps_adopt.
(ps_inited && slider22 > 0 && slider26 == 1) ? (
  pv_src = (slider22 - 1) + pv_src_semis(slider22, slider23, slider24);
  slider25 != pv_last_tgt ? (
    slider27 = slider25 - pv_src; slider_automate(slider27);
  ) : (slider27 != pv_last_tval || slider22 != pv_last_src || slider24 != pv_last_sf || slider23 != pv_last_sfu) ? (
    slider25 = max(0, min(127, floor(slider27 + pv_src + 0.5))); slider_automate(slider25);
  );
);
// The cut blocks (2026-09-16): the note name and the value follow each other, and a mode switch
// converts the value. Never on a slot switch -- the trackers are refreshed after the slot block.
(ps_inited && last_cap_sel == cap_sel) ? (
  slider37 != lc_last_mode ? ( slider39 = cut_conv(slider39, lc_last_mode, slider37); slider38 = cut_note(slider37, slider39); sliderchange(-1); ) :
  slider38 != lc_last_note ? ( slider39 = cut_from_note(slider37, slider38); sliderchange(-1); ) :
  slider39 != lc_last_val  ? ( slider38 = cut_note(slider37, slider39); sliderchange(-1); );
  slider42 != hc_last_mode ? ( slider44 = cut_conv(slider44, hc_last_mode, slider42); slider43 = cut_note(slider42, slider44); sliderchange(-1); ) :
  slider43 != hc_last_note ? ( slider44 = cut_from_note(slider42, slider43); sliderchange(-1); ) :
  slider44 != hc_last_val  ? ( slider43 = cut_note(slider42, slider44); sliderchange(-1); );
);
slider_show(slider25, slider22 > 0 && slider26 == 1);
// The first @slider after a load is NOT a slot switch, however it looks. A
// project saved with Capture slot 3 arrives with last_cap_slot still 0 from
// @init, and treating that as a switch loads slot 3's banks over the sliders --
// which on a v1 project are empty defaults, so the project's real Texture,
// Denoise and Pitch were destroyed before anything could read them. The values
// on screen are what the project saved; take them as given and stamp the slot.
(last_cap_sel != cap_sel && !is_first_slider) ? (
  // Every per-slot value banks with the slot, same as Capture point does.
  // Leaving All saves nothing: All holds nothing of its own.
  !last_cap_all ? (
    slot_voicedb[last_cap_slot] = slider61;
    slot_texture[last_cap_slot] = slider20 * 0.01;
    slot_spread[last_cap_slot] = slider32;
    slot_pitch[last_cap_slot] = slider27;
    slot_width[last_cap_slot] = slider35;
    slot_lowcut[last_cap_slot] = slider39;
    slot_denoise[last_cap_slot] = slider36;
    slot_fadein[last_cap_slot]  = slider12;                 // fade in
    slot_linger[last_cap_slot]  = slider13;                 // hold
    slot_xfade[last_cap_slot]   = slider14;                 // fade out
    slot_gap[last_cap_slot]     = slider15;                 // gap after
    slot_xfadeon[last_cap_slot] = slider18 > 0.5 ? 1 : 0;   // crossfade into next
    slot_mute[last_cap_slot]    = slider19 > 0.5 ? 1 : 0;   // mute
    slot_cappoint[last_cap_slot] = slider3 * 0.01;          // save the slot we're leaving
    slot_capavg[last_cap_slot] = max(1, min(CAPAVG_MAX, slider4));
    slot_ot_harm[last_cap_slot]  = max(0, min(NHARM, slider47));
    slot_ot_depth[last_cap_slot] = max(0, min(48, slider48));
    slot_srcnote[last_cap_slot] = slider22; slot_srcfine[last_cap_slot] = slider24; slot_srcfunit[last_cap_slot] = slider23;
    slot_tgtnote[last_cap_slot] = slider25; slot_tunit[last_cap_slot] = slider26;
    slot_fine[last_cap_slot] = slider29; slot_funit[last_cap_slot] = slider28;
    slot_grain[last_cap_slot] = slider21; slot_hicut[last_cap_slot] = slider44; slot_tmunit[last_cap_slot] = slider11;
    slot_spmode[last_cap_slot] = slider31; slot_spfunit[last_cap_slot] = slider33; slot_spfine[last_cap_slot] = slider34;
    slot_lcmode[last_cap_slot] = slider37; slot_lcfunit[last_cap_slot] = slider40; slot_lcfine[last_cap_slot] = slider41;
    slot_hcmode[last_cap_slot] = slider42; slot_hcfunit[last_cap_slot] = slider45; slot_hcfine[last_cap_slot] = slider46;
  );
  slider12 = slot_fadein[cap_slot];
  slider13 = slot_linger[cap_slot];
  slider14 = slot_xfade[cap_slot];
  slider15 = slot_gap[cap_slot];
  slider18 = slot_xfadeon[cap_slot] > 0.5 ? 1 : 0;
  slider19 = slot_mute[cap_slot] > 0.5 ? 1 : 0;
  slider61 = slot_voicedb[cap_slot];
  slider20 = slot_texture[cap_slot] * 100;
  slider32 = slot_spread[cap_slot];
  slider27 = slot_pitch[cap_slot];
  slider35 = slot_width[cap_slot];
  slider39 = slot_lowcut[cap_slot];
  slider36 = slot_denoise[cap_slot];
  slider22 = slot_srcnote[cap_slot]; slider24 = slot_srcfine[cap_slot]; slider23 = slot_srcfunit[cap_slot];
  slider25 = slot_tgtnote[cap_slot]; slider26 = slot_tunit[cap_slot];
  slider29 = slot_fine[cap_slot]; slider28 = slot_funit[cap_slot];
  slider21 = slot_grain[cap_slot]; slider44 = slot_hicut[cap_slot]; slider11 = slot_tmunit[cap_slot];
  slider31 = slot_spmode[cap_slot]; slider33 = slot_spfunit[cap_slot]; slider34 = slot_spfine[cap_slot];
  slider37 = slot_lcmode[cap_slot]; slider40 = slot_lcfunit[cap_slot]; slider41 = slot_lcfine[cap_slot];
  slider42 = slot_hcmode[cap_slot]; slider45 = slot_hcfunit[cap_slot]; slider46 = slot_hcfine[cap_slot];
  slider38 = cut_note(slider37, slider39); slider43 = cut_note(slider42, slider44);
  sliderchange(-1);   // refresh the displayed values; NOT slider_automate

  slider3 = slot_cappoint[cap_slot] * 100;         // load the slot we're entering (All shows Slot 1's)
